OS Security & Protection

Welcome to the final major chapter of Operating Systems 🎉

By now you know:

✅ Processes
✅ Threads
✅ Scheduling
✅ Memory Management
✅ Virtual Memory
✅ File Systems
✅ I/O Systems

Now the question is:

🤔 How does the OS protect data, users, files, passwords, and the system itself?

Answer:

🔐 Security & Protection
🧠 First Understand the Difference

This is a very common interview question.

Protection vs Security

Many students think both are the same.

❌ They are different.

🛡 Protection

Protection means:

Controlling access
inside the system.

Question:

Which user can access what?

Example:

Your file:

Resume.pdf

should not be editable by another user.

Protection handles this.

🔐 Security

Security means:

Protecting system from
external attacks.

Examples:

👨‍💻 Hacker

🦠 Virus

🐛 Worm

🎣 Phishing

Easy Analogy

House Example:

Protection
Which family member
can enter which room?
Security
Stop thieves
from entering house.
Comparison
Protection	Security
Internal control	External defense
User permissions	Attack prevention
Access management	Threat management
1️⃣ Authentication

One of the most important concepts.

Definition

Authentication verifies:

Who are you?

Examples:

🔑 Password

📱 OTP

👆 Fingerprint

🙂 Face Unlock

Real-Life Example

ATM asks:

Enter PIN

PIN proves identity.

This is:

Authentication
Authentication Factors
Something You Know

Examples:

Password
PIN
Something You Have

Examples:

Phone
Smart Card
OTP Device
Something You Are

Examples:

Fingerprint
Face
Retina
Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)

Uses multiple factors.

Example:

Password
+
OTP

Much safer.

2️⃣ Authorization

Interview favorite.

Authentication asks:

Who are you?

Authorization asks:

What can you do?

Example:

Login successful.

Now OS checks:

Can user delete file?
Can user install software?
Can user access admin panel?

This is:

Authorization
Easy Example

Airport:

Authentication

Passport check.

Authorization

Can you enter VIP lounge?

Authentication vs Authorization
Authentication	Authorization
Identity Verification	Permission Verification
Who are you?	What can you access?
First Step	Second Step
3️⃣ Access Control Matrix

Important theory topic.

Imagine:

Users:

Alice
Bob
Admin

Resources:

File1
File2
Printer

Matrix:

User	File1	File2	Printer
Alice	Read	Read	Print
Bob	Read/Write	No Access	Print
Admin	Full	Full	Full

This table is called:

Access Control Matrix

Purpose:

Who can do what?
4️⃣ Access Control Lists (ACL)

Very common in modern systems.

Instead of storing:

User → Resources

Store:

Resource → Users

Example

File:

Resume.pdf

ACL:

Lucky → Read Write

Admin → Full

Guest → Read Only

Used in many file systems.

5️⃣ Capabilities

Alternative to ACL.

ACL:

File knows users.

Capabilities:

User knows permissions.

Example

User token:

Read File1

Write File2

Print

This token is called:

Capability
6️⃣ Malware

Extremely Important.

Definition

Malware =

Malicious Software

Created to:

❌ Damage systems

❌ Steal data

❌ Spy on users

❌ Encrypt files

Types:

🦠 Virus

🐛 Worm

🐴 Trojan

👻 Rootkit

💰 Ransomware

7️⃣ Virus

Most famous malware.

Definition

A Virus is malicious code that attaches itself to another file or program.

Example:

Game.exe

contains hidden virus.

When program runs:

Virus executes
Characteristics

✅ Needs Host Program

✅ Spreads via files

Real-Life Analogy

Virus is like:

Infected passenger
inside a bus

Needs carrier.

8️⃣ Worm

Interview favorite.

Definition

A Worm is a standalone malware that spreads automatically across networks.

Difference:

Virus:

Needs Host

Worm:

No Host Needed

Example:

One infected PC.

Worm spreads to:

PC1
PC2
PC3
PC4

automatically.

Real-Life Analogy

Virus:

Needs vehicle

Worm:

Can walk by itself
Virus vs Worm
Virus	Worm
Needs Host	Standalone
User action often needed	Automatic spread
Slower spread	Faster spread
9️⃣ Trojan Horse

Very important.

Definition

Malware disguised as legitimate software.

Example:

Free Game

Actually:

Steals Passwords

User installs willingly.

Real-Life Analogy

Gift Box 🎁

Looks good outside.

Danger inside.

🔟 Rootkit

Advanced malware.

Purpose:

Hide malware
from OS and antivirus.

Very difficult to detect.

Often gains:

Administrator Access
1️⃣1️⃣ Ransomware

Modern cyber threat.

Example:

All files become:

Encrypted

Message:

Pay Money
or lose files.

This attack is called:

Ransomware
1️⃣2️⃣ Encryption

Extremely Important.

Definition

Encryption converts:

Readable Data
      ↓
Unreadable Data

Example:

Original:

Hello

Encrypted:

X7A92Q

Only authorized users can decrypt.

Why Encryption?

Protect:

🔑 Passwords

🏦 Banking Data

💬 Messages

📄 Documents

Easy Analogy

Locking a box.

Data:

Letter

inside box.

Encryption:

Lock

Decryption:

Key
1️⃣3️⃣ Firewalls

Very important.

Definition

A Firewall monitors and controls network traffic.

Think:

Security Guard

for network.

Example:

Incoming Request:

Allowed?
Blocked?

Firewall decides.

Real-Life Analogy

Gate Security at Society.

Allowed Visitors:

✅ Enter

Unknown Visitors:

❌ Block

1️⃣4️⃣ Secure Boot

Modern OS feature.

Purpose:

Ensure trusted software
starts during boot.

When laptop starts:

OS verifies:

Bootloader
Kernel
Drivers

If modified maliciously:

Boot blocked

Prevents boot-time malware.

